<p>
  The crested anole was first detected in Miami in the mid-1970s, carried over
  from Puerto Rico. It looks and behaves so much like the Cuban brown anole that
  the two are easy to mix up, yet they evolved independently on different islands.
  That near-match is what makes them so interesting to us. What happens when two
  species that fill the same role meet for the first time?
</p>

<h2>Watching two species sort themselves out</h2>
<p>
  We got to see it happen. In late 2017 crested anoles reached Lizard Island, our
  long-term study site in Miami, where we had already been measuring natural
  selection on the resident brown anoles for two years. Within four years crested
  anoles made up 66% of all adult anoles on the island, while brown anoles fell
  from 75% to 18%.
</p>
<p>
  Across Miami, where the two species live together, crested anoles perch higher
  and brown anoles move lower and onto the ground, each shifting away from the
  other. On Lizard Island, selection on brown anoles changed after crested anoles
  arrived, favoring individuals with
  relatively longer hind limbs. Taken together, this gave us a rare direct look at
  character displacement, the process by which competing species evolve apart, as
  it unfolded. The details are on our
  <a href="/research/lizard-island/">Lizard Island page</a>.
</p>

<h2>Small surprises</h2>
<p>
  Watching one species closely for years turns up oddities. In October 2017 at
  Fairchild Tropical Botanic Garden we saw an adult male crested anole, about 6 cm
  from snout to vent, eating a smaller member of its own species: the first record
  of cannibalism in crested anoles in Florida.
</p>
